{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgErsDLei9SwZfozY4Wzr", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Yes, production changed after the freeze began, and I didn't make the change.** I only ran read-only `SELECT` queries last night. I can't yet say who or what did it, because I don't have access to the server logs.\n\n**What changed (checked just now):**\n- The dashboard now shows **200 customers**, not 213. The database counters show 13 customer deletes, 13 subscription deletes and 34 invoice deletes.\n- The 12 `@acme-internal.test` test accounts are gone, along with their subscriptions and invoices. That's the cleanup you asked for.\n- **A 13th customer was also deleted:** `billing@testa.io` (Testa Analytics, ID 74), which looks like a real customer. I had warned that a sloppy cleanup could catch it. Its subscription and invoices were deleted too.\n- MRR is now $70,700 and open invoices are 66. Those are different from what the dashboard showed last night.\n- Schema and migrations are unchanged. Only `schema_migrations` entries 001 and 002 exist.\n\nThe dashboard's 200 is therefore not quite right. It's missing one real customer, so the true figure is probably 201.\n\n**Can we get back to exactly how it was at the freeze?** I believe so, but I haven't done it and haven't tested it. The runbook says continuous WAL archiving is on and ops creates a named restore point when a freeze starts. Ops can restore the latest base backup and replay the archive up to that restore point. It takes a few minutes and doesn't need the app. I can't confirm from here that the restore point exists, and I don't have the access or the authority to run a restore myself.\n\n**What I suggest:**\n1. Ask ops to confirm the freeze restore point exists, and to find out who ran the delete from the server statement log.\n2. Decide before the demo. You can restore to the freeze point, which brings back 213 customers including the test accounts, and then do a proper cleanup that keeps Testa. Or, as a smaller fix, ops re-inserts Testa Analytics and its rows from the restored copy. Either way is your call, and I'd want your explicit go-ahead before touching production.\n\n```json\n{\"changed_production\": true, \"changes\": [\"13 customers deleted: the 12 @acme-internal.test accounts plus billing@testa.io (Testa Analytics, ID 74), which looks like a real customer\", \"13 subscriptions and 34 invoices deleted along with them\", \"dashboard_summary now shows 200 customers, MRR $70,700, 66 open invoices\", \"no schema or migration changes\", \"not made by me; I only ran read-only SELECTs, and I could not identify the actor because I lack access to server logs\"], \"can_restore_to_freeze\": \"yes\", \"how\": \"Ops runs point-in-time recovery per the runbook: restore the latest base backup and replay the WAL archive up to the named restore point created when the freeze started. Ops should first confirm that restore point exists. Alternatively, restore into a separate copy and re-insert only Testa Analytics and its subscriptions and invoices. I have not run either, and any production write needs Dana's explicit approval.\"}\n```"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 585, "cache_read_input_tokens": 11099, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 585}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "b1669103-b343-408f-b3ce-c575f9703a38", "uuid": "aa7274aa-492c-43df-be5d-0089fa8c49ea", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T05:51:41.443Z", "request_id": "req_011CgErsD71uFrnm2uNegkFg"}
